{"id":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103251","venue":"j-a11637a2e7","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"Uncertainty quantification and propagation in lithium-ion battery electrodes using bayesian convolutional neural networks","authors":["Chance Norris","Abhinand Ayyaswamy","Bairav S. Vishnugopi","Carianne Martinez","Scott A. Roberts","Partha P. Mukherjee"],"author_ids":["https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4196-6771","https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7900-7261"],"abstract":null,"keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103251","pages":"103251","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103251","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-03","source":"crossref.org","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Energy Storage Materials","issn":"2405-8297","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_abstract","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":null,"ccf_paper_status":null,"dblp_key":null,"dblp_toc":null,"booktitle":null,"publisher_url":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2405829724000783","ai4s_hits":[],"ai4s_reason":"taxonomy_rule","ai4s_tags":["AI for 物质科学"],"ai4s_keywords":["battery","uncertainty quantification"],"ai4s_tagging":{"taxonomy_version":"1.2","basis":"metadata_rules","status":"needs_review","evidence":{"AI for 物质科学":["battery"]},"method_evidence":["uncertainty quantification"]}},
{"id":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103270","venue":"j-a11637a2e7","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"Exploiting domain knowledge to reduce data requirements for battery health monitoring","authors":["Jinpeng Tian","Liang Ma","Tieling Zhang","Te Han","Weijie Mai","C.Y. Chung"],"author_ids":["https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2816-327X"],"abstract":"Rechargeable batteries are becoming increasingly significant in decarbonising the world. For their widespread usage, to monitor and predict the battery health status has been essential. Although machine learning has the potential to tackle this issue, considerable degradation tests are required for model training, leading to prohibitive costs and labour. Here, we introduce a novel approach to constructing health monitoring models by fusing battery degradation knowledge with deep learning, using a substantially reduced amount of degradation data. We employ a lightweight and interpretable model to produce synthetic charging curves from highly limited realistic data. Subsequently, a transfer learning technique is implemented to train a convolutional neural network using both types of data and alleviate their gap. By employing only 8 realistic charging curves to develop the model, the method can precisely estimate the maximum and remaining capacities from 300 mV charging segments. The root mean square errors for these estimations are below 12.42 mAh. Additional 50 validation cases confirm that the proposed method can not only reduce the required degradation data but also shorten the input window length. Furthermore, it can be generalised and applied to different battery types under different operating conditions. This work highlights the promise of employing domain expertise to significantly decrease the amount of battery testing required for monitoring battery health.","keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103270","pages":"103270","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103270","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-03","source":"crossref.org+openaire.eu","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Energy Storage Materials","issn":"2405-8297","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_article_type","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":null,"ccf_paper_status":null,"dblp_key":null,"dblp_toc":null,"booktitle":null,"publisher_url":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2405829724000977","ai4s_hits":[],"ai4s_reason":"taxonomy_rule","ai4s_tags":["AI for 物质科学"],"ai4s_keywords":["battery","transfer learning"],"ai4s_tagging":{"taxonomy_version":"1.2","basis":"metadata_rules","status":"needs_review","evidence":{"AI for 物质科学":["battery"]},"method_evidence":["transfer learning"]}},
{"id":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103343","venue":"j-a11637a2e7","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"Physics-informed machine learning for battery degradation diagnostics: A comparison of state-of-the-art methods","authors":["Sina Navidi","Adam Thelen","Tingkai Li","Chao Hu"],"author_ids":["https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9228-7675"],"abstract":"Monitoring the health of lithium-ion batteries' internal components as they age is crucial for optimizing cell design and usage control strategies. However, quantifying component-level degradation typically involves aging many cells and destructively analyzing them throughout the aging test, limiting the scope of quantifiable degradation to the test conditions and duration. Fortunately, recent advances in physics-informed machine learning (PIML) for modeling and predicting the battery state of health demonstrate the feasibility of building models to predict the long-term degradation of a lithium-ion battery cell's major components using only short-term aging test data by leveraging physics. In this paper, we present four approaches for building physics-informed machine learning models and comprehensively compare them, considering accuracy, complexity, ease-of-implementation, and their ability to extrapolate to untested conditions. We delve into the details of each physics-informed machine learning method, providing insights specific to implementing them on small battery aging datasets. Our study utilizes long-term cycle aging data from 24 implantable-grade lithium-ion cells subjected to varying temperatures and C-rates over four years. This paper aims to facilitate the selection of an appropriate physics-informed machine learning method for predicting long-term degradation in lithium-ion batteries, using short-term aging data while also providing insights about when to choose which method for general predictive purposes.","keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103343","pages":"103343","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ensm.2024.103343","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-04","source":"crossref.org+openaire.eu","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Energy Storage Materials","issn":"2405-8297","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_article_type","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":null,"ccf_paper_status":null,"dblp_key":null,"dblp_toc":null,"booktitle":null,"publisher_url":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S2405829724001703","ai4s_hits":["physics-informed"],"ai4s_reason":"keyword","ai4s_tags":["AI for 数理科学","AI for 物质科学"],"ai4s_keywords":["physics-informed learning","battery"],"ai4s_tagging":{"taxonomy_version":"1.2","basis":"metadata_rules","status":"needs_review","evidence":{"AI for 数理科学":["physics-informed learning"],"AI for 物质科学":["battery"]},"method_evidence":["physics-informed learning"]}},
